<ProseLayout
  title="History"
  description="A brief history of the Village of Midvale, Ohio, from its 1888 platting to today."
  eyebrow="About"
  intro="Platted in 1888 in the Tuscarawas River valley."
>
  <p>
    The Village of Midvale was platted in 1888 and incorporated soon after,
    taking its name from its location at the midpoint between New Philadelphia
    (the Tuscarawas County seat) and Uhrichsville. The village sits in the
    Tuscarawas River valley and crosses portions of three townships: Goshen,
    Mill, and Warwick.
  </p>

  <p>
    Today, Midvale covers about three-quarters of a square mile and is home to
    roughly 700 residents. The village operates its own police department,
    contracts with the Village of Dennison for fire protection and Smith
    Ambulance Service for emergency medical services, and is currently
    transitioning its water utility to Aqua Ohio under a 2024 asset purchase
    agreement.
  </p>

  <p>
    Children in the village attend schools in the Indian Valley Local School
    District. Midvale Elementary School (kindergarten through fifth grade) is
    located within the village and serves about 400 students. Indian Valley
    Middle School is in the nearby village of Tuscarawas, and Indian Valley
    High School is in Gnadenhutten.
  </p>

  <p>
    Midvale Speedway, on the eastern edge of the village, has been a community
    institution for generations and continues to draw racing fans throughout
    the season.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    For deeper village history &mdash; photographs, oral histories, and stories
    about the people and places that shaped Midvale &mdash; visit the
    community-run history project at
    <a href="https://midvaleohiohistory.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">midvaleohiohistory.com</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
